    macroTendencyEn: "Italy: the theoretical recovery of ancient Roman architecture systematized architecture as a learned discipline. Brunelleschi’s dome marked a technical breakthrough, and Palladio’s architectural books had wide influence. Japanese archipelago: as warrior culture matured, shoin-zukuri was established. The development of the tea ceremony produced the distinctive grass-hut tea house.",
    westernDetailEn: "Brunelleschi’s dome for Florence Cathedral (1420–1436) used a double-shell structure without full centering. Bramante’s Tempietto (1502) exemplifies Renaissance ideal proportion. Palladio’s Villa Rotonda (1567–1592) is a model of the symmetrical plan.",
    eastAsianDetailEn: "The Dōjinsai at Tōgudō, Ginkaku-ji (1486), is an early shoin-zukuri example, with a four-and-a-half-tatami study. Tea houses such as Jo-an and Taian created spaces calibrated to the seated eye, including features such as the nijiriguchi crawl-through entrance and tokonoma alcove. Jurakudai (1587), Toyotomi Hideyoshi’s administrative residence, combined castle and mansion architecture.",
    footnoteEn: "Renaissance architecture took ancient Roman architectural treatises, especially Vitruvius’s Ten Books on Architecture, as models. In Japan, shoin-zukuri and sukiya-zukuri became the two major currents of early modern architecture.",
    examKeywordsEn: ["Brunelleschi’s dome", "Tempietto", "Villa Rotonda", "Shoin-zukuri (Dōjinsai)", "Tea houses (Jo-an and Taian)"],
    century: "15th–16th Century",
