    macroTendencyEn: "Western Europe: architecture moved from Baroque’s dynamic, dramatic space toward Neoclassicism’s restrained rationality. In the eighteenth century, preservation debates began and Winckelmann’s doctrine of imitation of antiquity directed Neoclassicism. Japanese archipelago: under the Tokugawa shogunate, shrine-mausoleum architecture in the gongen-zukuri mode reached an ornamental peak, while sukiya-zukuri, exemplified by Katsura Imperial Villa, pursued a spare beauty.",
    westernDetailEn: "Borromini’s San Carlo alle Quattro Fontane (1638–1641) typifies Baroque dynamic space. Soufflot’s Panthéon in Paris (1758–1790) is a major Neoclassical work. In the later eighteenth century, architecture began to treat styles as historically relative.",
    eastAsianDetailEn: "Nikkō Tōshō-gū, rebuilt in 1636 under the third shogun Tokugawa Iemitsu, represents the apogee of gongen-zukuri; its Yōmeimon gate bears more than 150 carvings. Katsura Imperial Villa, dating to the mid-seventeenth century, is a leading sukiya-zukuri work, prized for its spare and refined spatial composition.",
    footnoteEn: "In eighteenth-century Europe, debate over architectural preservation and restoration became substantive, establishing an intellectual foundation for later conservation charters. The maturation of Japanese townspeople’s culture also influenced architecture.",
